{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Ring.Defs","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":7,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.ratios_are_scale_free","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.ratios_are_scale_free","kind":"theorem","line":97,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L97-L106","doc":"**The single-term consequence.** With the saturating half inert, one term\ncarries all three cumulants, so its `b₃/b₂` and `b₄/b₂` are fixed by its shape\nalone — no coefficient can move them. Stated as: scaling a term by `γ` leaves\nboth ratios unchanged. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.cap_two_b3_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.cap_two_b3_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":93,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L93-L95","doc":"The `k = 2` cap gives bond counts `1, 3, 4` and still contributes nothing to\n`b₃` — only `b₄` sees it, and with the wrong sign (`−2`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_contributes_nothing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_contributes_nothing","kind":"theorem","line":85,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L85-L91","doc":"Both at once: the complete-graph bond count — which is what *any* cap\n`k ≥ 3` reduces to on `A ≤ 4` — contributes nothing to either cumulant, so a\nsaturating coordination term has **no signature in the window where `b₃` and `b₄`\nare measured**. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_b4_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_b4_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":81,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L81-L83","doc":"And exactly zero to `b₄`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_b3_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.complete_b3_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":75,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L75-L79","doc":"**A degree cap at `k ≥ 3` is invisible to both cumulants.** For `A ≤ k+1` the\ncap does not bind, so the bond count is the complete-graph count `1, 3, 6` at\n`A = 2, 3, 4` — and that contributes exactly zero to `b₃`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.b4Contrib","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.b4Contrib","kind":"def","line":72,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L72-L73","doc":"The `b₄` contribution of a term taking values `f₂, f₃, f₄`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.b3Contrib","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.Mass.SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.b3Contrib","kind":"def","line":69,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/Mass/SaturationInvisibleToCumulants.lean#L69-L70","doc":"The `b₃` contribution of a term taking values `f₂, f₃` at `A = 2, 3`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}